@ 2 0 0 1dbb65a35d0414830b296d4fbe6d1103
+ #### Searching what is sorted
+
+ Elements which are kept in order can be searched in logarithmic time. But such a search is only
+ right if the elements really are sorted, and sorted by the very order the search compares with.
+ A `Sorting` holds the two together: it is made from a collection and the key to sort by, keeps
+ the elements in that order, and searches by it.
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ case class Player(name: Text, age: Int)
+
+ val players = List(Player(t"Orsino", 31), Player(t"Viola", 19), Player(t"Feste", 44))
+ val cast = Sorting(players)(_.age)
+
+ Out.println(cast.find(31).let(_.name).inspect)
+ Out.println(cast.find(25).inspect)
+ Out.println(cast.locate(19).let(_.n0).inspect)
+ ```
+
+ `find` gives the element with a key, and `locate` its position, each `Unset` if there is none.
+ `lowerBound` is the position at which a key is, or would be inserted, and `upperBound` the
+ position after the last element with that key, so the two span the elements whose keys are
+ equal:
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ val years = Sorting(List(31, 19, 44, 31, 28))
+
+ Out.println(years.lowerBound(31).n0.show)
+ Out.println(years.upperBound(31).n0.show)
+ Out.println(years.lowerBound(30).n0.show)
+ ```
+
+ With no key given, the elements are sorted by their own comparison. The sorted elements
+ themselves are the `Sorting`'s `elements`.
+

@ 3 0 0 1e0a4f66471a12e0081283fb97189a28
+ `intersect` and `except` are also defined for a `List`, a `Sequence` and a `Chain`, which they
+ treat as multisets. Each element of the operand accounts for at most one equal element of the
+ receiver, and the receiver's order is kept. So `except` counts what one list has beyond another,
+ with a repeated element counted once for each extra occurrence:
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ val before = List(t"warn", t"warn", t"note")
+ val after = List(t"warn", t"warn", t"warn", t"note", t"halt")
+
+ Out.println(after.except(before).inspect)
+ Out.println(after.intersect(List(t"warn", t"halt", t"halt")).inspect)
+ ```
+

@ 2 0 0 174f328f9d2f041f17651133bd3462c0
+ A map with many entries is quicker to fill in place than to grow one `define` at a time.
+ `Map.build` lends a block a builder to write to, and gives back an ordinary `Map` when the
+ block ends:
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ val court = Map.build[Text, Int]: map =>
+   map(t"Lear") = 80
+   map(t"Kent") = 48
+   map(t"Gloucester") = 60
+
+ Out.println(court.at(t"Kent").inspect)
+ Out.println((court == ages.define(t"Gloucester", 60)).inspect)
+ ```
+
+ Only the builder can write to the map, and the builder cannot leave its block: keeping a
+ reference to it for later is a compile error. So the map is immutable from the moment `build`
+ returns, and equal to any other map with the same entries, however that one was made.
+
+ #### Keys told apart another way
+
+ Two keys of a map are the same key when they are equal. That is a policy, and `Map.build` takes
+ it from the context: an `Equatable`, which says when two keys are the same, and a `Hashable`
+ which agrees with it. For every type they are `==` and the ordinary hash, unless another is in
+ scope.
+
+ The usual reason to choose another is to tell keys apart by *which object* they are. Notes
+ attached to the nodes of an immutable tree must not merge two nodes which happen to be equal,
+ and a large node should not be hashed through on every lookup.
+ `equalities.identityEquality` chooses that:
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ case class Node(label: Text)
+
+ val first = Node(t"leaf")
+ val second = Node(t"leaf")
+
+ val depths =
+   import equalities.identityEquality
+
+   Map.build[Node, Int]: map =>
+     map(first) = 1
+     map(second) = 2
+
+ Out.println(depths.at(first).inspect)
+ Out.println(depths.at(second).inspect)
+ Out.println(depths.at(Node(t"leaf")).inspect)
+ ```
+
+ The map keeps the policy it was built with. A key added to it with `define`, or removed with
+ `omit`, is compared the same way, and so are the keys of a map added to it with `+`. A map made
+ from it with *other* keys, by a conversion or a filter, is built afresh by whichever policy is
+ in scope there.
+
+ ### Maps with keys known in advance
+
+ A map whose keys are all written where it is made can be read faster still. `Map.fixed` finds a
+ *perfect hash* of its keys while the code is compiled: a small calculation which sends each key
+ to a slot of its own. A read is then that calculation and one comparison, whether or not the
+ key is there:
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ val cast = Map.fixed(t"Lear" -> 80, t"Kent" -> 48, t"Gloucester" -> 60, t"Edgar" -> 28)
+
+ Out.println(cast.at(t"Edgar").inspect)
+ Out.println(cast.at(t"Fool").inspect)
+ Out.println((cast == court.define(t"Edgar", 28)).inspect)
+ ```
+
+ The keys must be literals: texts, `Int`s, `Long`s or `Char`s, all of one kind, and each given
+ once. The values can be any expressions. A fixed map is an ordinary `Map` to everything which
+ uses it, and `define` and `omit` work on it as on any other.
+
+ A map which cannot be fixed does not compile, so a map made with `fixed` is never a slower one
+ in disguise. Giving a key twice is one such error:
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ compile
+ errors show
+ ##
+ Map.fixed(t"Lear" -> 80, t"Kent" -> 48, t"Lear" -> 81)
+ ```
+
+ A key which is not a literal is another, as are keys of different kinds, and two texts which
+ happen to share a hash code. A map of more than 1,024 entries is too large to write out this
+ way, and is better filled with `Map.build`.
+
+ When the keys are only known once the program is running, `optimize` gives an existing map the
+ same treatment. It finds the hash then, and compiles it with the Scala compiler:
+
+ ```scala
+ import murmuration.optimize
+ ```
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ typecheck
+ ##
+ val quick = court.optimize
+ ```
+
+ The compiler must therefore be on the classpath when the program runs, which is why `optimize`
+ comes from a module of its own, in the `staged` bundle, and only on the JVM. Compiling takes
+ about a tenth of a second for each map, and several seconds for the first, so it suits a map
+ which is made once and read a great many times.
+
+ It helps a map keyed by numbers most, which reads about twice as fast. A map keyed by text
+ reads faster when its keys are usually found, and slower when they usually are not, since a
+ text which is absent must still be compared with the one in its slot. A map of four entries or
+ fewer, or one whose keys are not all texts, all `Int`s, all `Long`s or all `Char`s, is given
+ back as it was.
+
